Report recovery checks bind to the supplied backend image
========================================================

scripts/check_report_recovery.py uses disposable containers, synthetic report
data, real PostgreSQL dumps/restores and a local TLS SMTP fixture. It does not
use customer backups, send external mail or contact managed hardware. Its
--work-dir must be a new absolute directory on a POSIX filesystem that enforces
0700 permissions. That directory contains synthetic credentials and database
dumps; publish only its report.json, never the complete working directory.

The helper resolves each supplied local image to an immutable image ID. After
migration, it reads the single Alembic head from that exact backend image and
requires the database's revision table to match. Empty, multiple, malformed
or mismatched revisions fail before report creation or mail delivery. Each
restored database must pass the same comparison before its recovery probe.
The report records only validated revisions and qualified image identities.

This replaces the previous fixed 018_report_recovery_epoch comparison. The
check now works with later release migrations, including the in-app outbox,
without treating a database left at the old revision as fully migrated.

The seven existing recovery cases remain unchanged: create an approved pending
notice; accept its original mail; demonstrate duplicate replay without an epoch
change; quarantine with a missing epoch; quarantine with a rotated epoch;
explicitly approve a new notice only; and preserve those outcomes after a
fresh process and empty cache. The deliberately unsafe replay is confined to
the synthetic fixture and explains why restoration requires an epoch change.

A passing revision comparison establishes migration-version alignment, not
complete physical schema equivalence, data integrity, customer restore time,
cross-host recovery or exactly-once delivery. These require separate evidence.
Multiple Alembic heads are refused until the release supplies a merged head
and migration path. No image is pulled implicitly by image-ID resolution.
